{
  "subtype": "faceted_decomposition",
  "concept": "operational_burden",
  "per_column": [
    {
      "name": "issue_category",
      "is_constitutive_facet": false,
      "role": "external_predictor",
      "rationale": "The category/type of issue is a driver or classifier of what generates burden, not an aspect OF the burden itself. Operational burden is constituted by facets like handling time, effort, volume, escalation, and reopen rate -- not by the taxonomy of the issue that causes it. It is a predictor of burden, failing the meronymy test."
    },
    {
      "name": "burden_score",
      "is_constitutive_facet": false,
      "role": "restatement",
      "rationale": "A single aggregate burden score is a restatement of the whole focus concept, not a decomposition into constitutive parts. A faceted decomposition requires breaking burden into non-overlapping component aspects; a composite score collapses the concept back into one number rather than carving it up."
    }
  ],
  "facet_columns": [],
  "non_facet_columns": ["issue_category", "burden_score"],
  "mutual_exclusivity": 0.0,
  "exhaustiveness": 0.0,
  "rationale": "Neither added column is a constitutive facet (part-of) of operational_burden. 'issue_category' is an external predictor/classifier of what causes burden, and 'burden_score' is a restatement of the whole concept rather than one of its parts. With no genuine facets present, the set does not carve the concept into non-overlapping aspects (mutual exclusivity trivially 0) and covers none of its constitutive dimensions such as time-on-task, escalation, reopens, or agent effort (exhaustiveness 0). The faceted decomposition claim is not realised."
}

